## TLDR Problem this solves: - A Redis blip made `/key/update` return 400 after the write landed - Callers retried updates that had already been applied - Six other key endpoints carried the same latent 400 How it solves it: - Cache eviction after a committed write is now best-effort - Each of the two caches is guarded on its own - What could not be evicted is logged at error level ## User Flow Before: an admin whose gateway has a Redis outage cannot tell whether a key update applied 1. They send POST https://litellm-domain/key/update with `{"key": "sk-...", "max_budget": 42}` 2. They get back HTTP 400 and `"Authentication Error, Redis circuit breaker is open — skipping async_delete_cache"`, so their tooling records the update as failed 3. They send GET https://litellm-domain/key/info?key=sk-... and see `"max_budget": 42.0`, which contradicts the error they just got 4. With the two answers disagreeing, they retry the update and get another 400 for a change that was already in place After: the same request reports the success it is, and the operator still learns what went stale 1. They send POST https://litellm-domain/key/update with the same body 2. They get back HTTP 200 carrying the updated key with `"max_budget": 42.0` 3. GET https://litellm-domain/key/info?key=sk-... agrees, so there is nothing to retry 4. The proxy log carries an error line naming the key whose cached copy could not be dropped, saying it keeps its previous settings until that entry expires ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [ ] My PR passes all CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, format, unit tests) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@gr