## TLDR Problem this solves: - `/v1/messages` drops a segment from `provider/vendor/model` deployment ids - Perplexity rejects the truncated id with HTTP 400 `Invalid model` - `/v1/chat/completions` and `/v1/responses` send the same deployment correctly - Ids like `perplexity/perplexity/sonar` silently reach the wrong Perplexity endpoint How it solves it: - `/v1/messages` hands its bridges the caller's id unresolved - Provider resolution then runs once instead of twice - The Responses bridge keeps its marker in the provider-qualified slot - `message_start` still reports the model as the provider names it ## User Flow Before: a developer whose gateway fronts a Perplexity deployment gets a 400 on `/v1/messages`, while the very same deployment answers on the other two routes 1. The proxy admin configures a deployment with model `perplexity/perplexity/kimi-k3` and exposes it as `multi-segment` 2. The developer sends POST `http://litellm-domain/v1/chat/completions` with `"model": "multi-segment"` and gets HTTP 200 with the reply 3. The developer sends the same prompt to POST `http://litellm-domain/v1/messages` with `"model": "multi-segment"` 4. That comes back HTTP 400 reading `Invalid model name passed in model=kimi-k3`, naming a model nobody configured or asked for 5. Deployments whose id has only two segments keep answering on `/v1/messages`, so the failure looks arbitrary and the developer has no way to tell which of their deployments are affected After: the same request succeeds, and the provider receives the id the admin actually configured 1. The proxy admin configures a deployment with model `perplexity/perplexity/kimi-k3` and exposes it as `multi-segment` 2. The developer sends POST `http://litellm-domain/v1/chat/completions` with `"model": "multi-segment"` and gets HTTP 200 with the reply 3. The developer sends the same prompt to POST `http://litellm-domain/v1/messages` with `"model": "multi-segment"` 4. That now comes back HTTP 200 with the reply, and Perplexity is asked for `perplexity/kimi-k3`, matching what `/v1/chat/completions` already sent 5. Two-segment deployments answer exactly as before, so all three routes agree on every deployment ## Relevant issues Fixes #37716 ## Linear ticket ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v`. Leave the suites (`make test-unit-*`, `make test-unit`) to CI: it finishes in ~15 minutes where a laptop takes an hour or more - [x] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [x] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA). ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Perplexity credentials were not available for this run, so the deployment's `api_base` points at a second litellm proxy standing in for Perplexity's endpoint, which in turn fronts a real billed Bedrock upstream. The stand-in was verified first to discriminate exactly the way the real endpoint does: `perplexity/kimi-k3` answers 200 and bare `kimi-k3` answers 400 `Invalid model name passed in model=kimi-k3`. The perplexity provider code path itself runs unmodified, and every case below is a real billed call Subject proxy on `:4711`, `qa_config.yaml`: ```yaml model_list: - model_name: multi-segment litellm_params: model: perplexity/perplexity/kimi-k3 api_base: http://localhost:4712 api_key: sk-1234 - model_name: plain-two-segment litellm_params: model: litellm_proxy/bedrock-converse-haiku-4-5 api_base: http://localhost:4000/v1 api_key: sk-1234 - model_name: openai-bridge litellm_params: model: openai/gpt-5.6-luna api_base: http://localhost:4712/v1 api_key: sk-1234 general_settings: master_key: sk-1234 ``` Stand-in endpoint on `:4712`, `qa_upstream.yaml`, backed by a real Bedrock deployment on `:4000`: ```yaml model_list: - model_name: perplexity/kimi-k3 litellm_params: model: litellm_proxy/bedrock-converse-haiku-4-5 api_base: http://localhost:4000/v1 api_key: sk-1234 - model_name: gpt-5.6-luna litellm_params: model: litellm_proxy/bedrock-converse-haiku-4-5 api_base: http://localhost:4000/v1 api_key: sk-1234 general_settings: master_key: sk-1234 ``` ### Before (1d7f675e52) #### Multi-segment deployment on /v1/messages 1. Run: ``` curl -s -w '
HTTP %{http_code}
' http://localhost:4711/v1/messages \ -H 'Authorization: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \ -d '{"model":"multi-segment","max_tokens":512,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Reply with exactly: pong"}]}' ``` 2. Observe a 400 naming a model that was never configured: ``` {"error": {"message": "litellm.BadRequestError: PerplexityException - /chat/completions: Invalid model name passed in model=kimi-k3. Call `/v1/models` to view available models for your key.. Received Model Group=multi-segment
