## TLDR Problem this solves: - A client's Anthropic OAuth token was forwarded to Bedrock and Vertex - It displaced each deployment's own credential, so every fallback there failed - The token also ended up in AWS and Google request logs How it solves it: - The OAuth credential is now scoped to Anthropic alone - Portable `anthropic-beta` and `anthropic-version` headers keep their existing scope - Provider-specific headers can now carry independently scoped entries ## User Flow ### Before The request dies the moment the gateway falls back off Anthropic, and the user's own subscription token is handed to AWS and Google on the way 1. The user points Claude Code at their gateway: base URL `https://litellm-domain`, their Anthropic subscription token (`sk-ant-oat...`) as the auth token, the gateway key in `x-litellm-api-key`, and the model set to `claude-primary` 2. The user runs `claude`, types "What is 2+2? Answer in one word." and presses Enter 3. Claude Code sends `POST https://litellm-domain/v1/messages` with `Authorization: Bearer sk-ant-oat...` and `"model": "claude-primary"` 4. The Anthropic-backed group is at its rate limit, so the gateway moves the request onto the configured `claude-bedrock` fallback 5. The user gets back `401`, and the body says `Available Model Group Fallbacks=['claude-bedrock']` followed by `Error doing the fallback: {"Message":"Invalid API Key format: Must start with pre-defined prefix"}` 6. Claude Code retries nine more times over about four minutes and forty seconds. Every attempt ends the same way 7. The screen settles on `Please run /login`. The user never gets an answer, even though the Bedrock deployment was healthy and reachable the entire time 8. The same user sending `"model": "claude-vertex"` to `POST https://litellm-domain/v1/messages` gets `401 UNAUTHENTICATED` with `ACCESS_TOKEN_TYPE_UNSUPPORTED` 9. Security consequence: that subscription token was transmitted in full to `bedrock-runtime.us-west-2.amazonaws.com` and to `us-east5-aiplatform.googleapis.com`. Anyone who can read request logs at either cloud provider, or at anything sitting between the gateway and them, can lift the token and spend the user's Anthropic subscription ### After The same request completes on the Bedrock fallback, and nothing but each deployment's own credential ever leaves the gateway 1. The user points Claude Code at their gateway: base URL `https://litellm-domain`, their Anthropic subscription token (`sk-ant-oat...`) as the auth token, the gateway key in `x-litellm-api-key`, and the model set to `claude-primary` 2. The user runs `claude`, types "What is 2+2? Answer in one word." and presses Enter 3. Claude Code sends `POST https://litellm-domain/v1/messages` with `Authorization: Bearer sk-ant-oat...` and `"model": "claude-primary"` 4. The Anthropic-backed group is at its rate limit, so the gateway moves the request onto the configured `claude-bedrock` fallback 5. The Bedrock call goes out under that deployment's own AWS credentials and the user gets back `200` with the assistant's reply 6. "Four" renders in Claude Code on the first attempt, with no retry ladder and no `Please run /login` 7. The same user sending `"model": "claude-vertex"` to `POST https://litellm-domain/v1/messages` gets `200` and the assistant's reply 8. Security consequence: the subscription token now reaches Anthropic and nothing else. AWS sees only the AWS credential, Google sees only the Google one, so there is no longer an Anthropic credential sitting in either cloud provider's request logs for someone else to find ## Root cause `add_provider_specific_headers_to_request` collected two very different things into one bucket: - portable Anthropic API headers (`anthropic-version`, `anthropic-beta`) - the client's `Authorization` header, when it looks like an Anthropic OAuth token It then tagged the whole bucket with one provider scope, `anthropic,bedrock,vertex_ai`. The comment there explains the intent, to send these only to "Anthropic-compatible providers". That is right for the API headers and wrong for the credential: Bedrock and Vertex serve Claude models, but they do not accept an Anthropic credential. Serving the same model family and accepting the same credential are different things, and the two were conflated Provider-specific headers are merged in last, so they land after the provider has already computed its own auth. What that produced depended on the provider, and on Bedrock it also depended on the route: The split is Bedrock's invoke route against its converse route. `/v1/messages` uses invoke; `/v1/chat/completions` and `/v1/responses` use converse - **Bedrock SigV4, invoke**: substitution. `base_aws_llm.py:1602` scans the merged headers case-insensitively for `authorization` and restores it over the computed signature, so the outbound request went out with `X-Amz-Date` present (signing had run) and a `Bearer` value sitting where the `AWS4-HMAC-SHA256` signature belonged. AWS answered `Invalid API Key format: Must start with pre-defined prefix` - **Bedrock `api_key` bearer, invoke**: duplication. Signing is short-circuited, so the client credential rode along as a second lowercase `authorization` beside the deployment's own capitalized one, and Bedrock picked the client's. A working 200 became the same 403, so this was never SigV4-specific - **Bedrock, converse**: unaffected either way. The restore check at `base_aws_llm.py:1476` tests `extra_headers` for the exact capitalized key `"Authorization"`, clients send it lowercased, so the check missed and the signature survived. `authorization` is in `SIGV4_COMPUTED_HEADERS`, so the add-back loop dropped the client value instead of forwarding it - **Vertex, all three routes**: duplication. No comparable filtering, so the bearer arrived as a second lowercase `authorization` next to the Google access token and Google picked the wrong one, rejecting with `ACCESS_TOKEN_TYPE_UNSUPPORTED` Worth stating plainly for reviewers: a regression test that only exercised Bedrock through `/v1/chat/completions` would have passed against the unfixed code while `/v1/messages` stayed broken Scoping the credential at the source cuts off all four, because the credential no longer resolves for `bedrock` or `vertex_ai` and never enters those headers in the first place ## Fix Scope the credential separately from the API headers `data["provider_specific_header"]` now carries either a single entry, as before, or a list of independently scoped entries. The portable API headers keep the `anthropic,bedrock,vertex_ai` scope they have today. The OAuth credential gets its own entry scoped to `anthropic` alone, so it still reaches Anthropic unchanged and never reaches anything else `ProviderSpecificHeaderUtils.get_provider_specific_headers` normalizes both shapes and merges only the entries whose provider list matches the resolved provider. Emitting a bare dict when there is only one entry keeps every existing caller and test working untouched The fix is at the source, in what gets tagged for which provider. The `_sign_request` incoming-authorization restore in `base_aws_llm.py` is deliberate (it dates to 6431af0678 and supports real `extra_headers` Authorization pass-through), so it is left alone and its behavior is pinned by a test instead `anthropic-beta` and `anthropic-version` forwarding is byte-for-byte unchanged. This PR is deliberately scoped to the credential and does not touch API header semantics ## Testing 20 tests added to `tests/test_litellm/proxy/test_litellm_pre_call_utils.py`, the mapped suite for the fix site, exercising the real producer and the real consumer together and calling the real `BaseAWSLLM()._sign_request`: - the OAuth credential never reaches bedrock, bedrock_converse, or vertex_ai, across three header casings - it still reaches anthropic unchanged, across three header casings - Bedrock SigV4 signing is unaffected, with and without a client OAuth header - a Bedrock deployment with its own `api_key` bearer keeps that bearer - a deliberately configured `extra_headers` Authorization still overrides SigV4, so pass-through signing is not regressed - Vertex sends exactly one Authorization header Four pre-existing tests encoded the old behavior and were updated to the new scoping Counts: 317 passed in the pre-call suite these 20 joined, 742 across the six affected suites, 200 in `tests/proxy_unit_tests/test_proxy_utils.py`, and 197 across `test_llm_http_handler.py` plus `test_base_aws_llm.py`. With the fix stashed, 11 of the 20 fail, so the regression coverage is real ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket Resolves LIT-5923 ## Pre-Submission checklist **Please complete all items before asking a LiteLLM maintainer to review your PR** - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v`. Leave the suites (`make test-unit-*`, `make test-unit`) to CI: it finishes in ~15 minutes where a laptop takes an hour or more - [x] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [x] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Delays in PR merge? If you're seeing a delay in your PR being merged, ping the LiteLLM Team on [Slack (#pr-review)](https://join.slack.com/t/litellmossslack/shared_invite/zt-3o7nkuyfr-p_kbNJj8taRfXGgQI1~YyA) ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Run e2e against a live proxy with real provider calls and no mocks, both halves driven by the same curl commands. Four deployments in one router config: `claude-primary` (Anthropic), `claude-bedrock-sigv4` (Bedrock, static AWS keys), `claude-bedrock-apikey` (Bedrock, `api_key` bearer), and `claude-vertex` (Vertex AI Claude). Every request also carries `x-litellm-api-key: Bearer sk-1234` The forwarded credential is a **synthetic** token, never a real subscription token, so no valid credential is ever transmitted to AWS or Google: ``` sk-ant-oat01-REPROLIT5923synthetic0000000000000000000000000000000000000000AA ``` Header values carry LiteLLM's own masking. `Be****AA` is the synthetic client bearer, `AW****xx` a real SigV4 signature, `Be****==` the Bedrock `api_key` bearer, and `Be****l9` / `Be****8F` / `Be****Ym` / `Be****jf` the Google service-account token. Each "Proxy sent" step reports what actually went out to the provider on that request Two things to read correctly before comparing the halves: - A leaking case shows **two** auth headers on one outbound request, the client's lowercase `authorization` next to the deployment's own capitalized `Authorization`. One header is the fixed state, two is the bug - On the converse routes the deployment's own bearer is emitted lowercase as `authorization: Be****==` on one half and capitalized on the other. Same value, same 200, not a leak. The client bearer masks to `Be****AA` and appears in neither No valid AWS SigV4 credential exists on the test machine, so the SigV4 cases return 403 on both halves and are carried by their header evidence rather than by status. The `api_key` and Vertex cases carry the status flips. On the Anthropic case both halves pin `"fallbacks":[]` so the now-working Bedrock fallback cannot mask Anthropic's own 401 The After half ran at `81cdf1a821`. The one commit after it, `b1b29e5c`, only moves test functions between test files and touches no production code, so this run still describes the tip ### Before (843e90c2f5) #### Bedrock SigV4 deployment via /v1/messages 1. Control, no client `Authorization`: `curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:$PORT/v1/messages -H 'x-litellm-api-key: Bearer sk-1234' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"model":"claude-bedrock-sigv4",...}'` 2. Returns `HTTP 403`, {"error":{"message":"{"message":"