## TLDR Problem this solves: - JWT `team_allowed_routes` only matched exact routes - Every new passthrough route needed a config change - A shared route prefix could not be granted once How it solves it: - Match trailing-wildcard patterns like `/internal-models/*` - Reuses the wildcard helper key-level `allowed_routes` already uses - Applies to `admin_allowed_routes` too ## User Flow Before: a platform team registers new internal passthrough endpoints on a schedule, and each one is a 403 for their developers until someone ships a config change 1. An admin registers a new passthrough endpoint at `/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages` 2. A developer authenticating with a team JWT sends POST https://litellm-domain/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages 3. They get back 403 and the call never reaches the model 4. Someone edits the proxy config to add that one exact route under `general_settings -> litellm_jwtauth -> team_allowed_routes`, restarts the proxy, and only then does the call work 5. The wildcard `/internal-models/*` looks like it should avoid all of this, but it is treated as a literal route name and matches nothing After: the prefix is granted once, and every endpoint registered under it works immediately 1. An admin sets `team_allowed_routes: ["/internal-models/*"]` once and restarts the proxy 2. An admin registers a new passthrough endpoint at `/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages` 3. A developer authenticating with a team JWT sends POST https://litellm-domain/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages 4. They get back 200 with the model response, with no config change for the new endpoint 5. The same team JWT calling a route outside the prefix, such as https://litellm-domain/anthropic-direct/v1/messages, still gets 403 ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket Resolves LIT-4885 ## Pre-Submission checklist - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v` - [x] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Shared setup, a live proxy with JWT auth on, one wildcard grant, and two Anthropic passthrough routes, one under the granted prefix and one outside it ```yaml general_settings: master_key: sk-1234 enable_jwt_auth: true litellm_jwtauth: team_ids_jwt_field: "team_ids" user_id_jwt_field: "sub" user_id_upsert: true team_allowed_routes: ["/internal-models/*"] pass_through_endpoints: - path: "/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages" target: "https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages" headers: x-api-key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY anthropic-version: "2023-06-01" - path: "/anthropic-direct/v1/messages" target: "https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages" headers: x-api-key: os.environ/ANTHROPIC_API_KEY anthropic-version: "2023-06-01" ``` The caller is a team JWT for `internal-team-1`, a team with `all-proxy-models`, and both cases send the same body ```bash JWT=$(cat team.jwt) BODY='{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","max_tokens":32,"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Say hello in 3 words"}]}' ``` ### Before (dad4c1a0fb) #### Route under the granted prefix 1. `curl -s -o out.json -w "%{http_code}
" -X POST http://localhost:4000/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages -H "Authorization: Bearer $JWT" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "$BODY"` 2. Output ``` 403 {"error":{"message":"No team has access to the requested model: claude-sonnet-4-5. Checked teams={'internal-team-1'}. Check `/models` to see all available models.","type":"auth_error","param":"None","code":"403"}} ``` #### Route outside the granted prefix 1. `curl -s -o out.json -w "%{http_code}
" -X POST http://localhost:4000/anthropic-direct/v1/messages -H "Authorization: Bearer $JWT" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "$BODY"` 2. Output ``` 403 {"error":{"message":"No team has access to the requested model: claude-sonnet-4-5. Checked teams={'internal-team-1'}. Check `/models` to see all available models.","type":"auth_error","param":"None","code":"403"}} ``` ### After (07416344cc) #### Route under the granted prefix 1. `curl -s -o out.json -w "%{http_code}
" -X POST http://localhost:4000/internal-models/anthropic/v1/messages -H "Authorization: Bearer $JWT" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "$BODY"` 2. Output, a real Anthropic response, no exact-route config entry for it ``` 200 {"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_011CeF5n4PZqyoEeapsvvype","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hello to you!"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","usage":{"input_tokens":14,...}} ``` #### Route outside the granted prefix 1. `curl -s -o out.json -w "%{http_code}
" -X POST http://localhost:4000/anthropic-direct/v1/messages -H "Authorization: Bearer $JWT" -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "$BODY"` 2. Output, still denied, so the grant stays scoped to the prefix ``` 403 {"error":{"message":"No team has access to the requested model: claude-sonnet-4-5. Checked teams={'internal-team-1'}. Check `/models` to see all available models.","type":"auth_error","param":"None","code":"403"}} ``` ## Type 🐛 Bug Fix ## Caveats (if any) - Only a trailing `*` is a wildcard, matching existing route matching - `/prefix/*` does not match the bare `/prefix` route ### Final Attestation - [x] The tests check the right things, including the edge cases, and regressions in the respective real-world customer use-cases are not possible after this PR Link to Devin session: https://app.devin.ai/sessions/f187516a5a924ceb9c833eaa7a699cc9

## TLDR Problem this solves: - SCIM group members matched an exact litellm user id only - An SSO-provisioned person is listed by email or subject - Since #37688 that mismatch fails the whole push with 500 - A value naming two accounts had no safe answer How it solves it: - One lookup across sso_user_id and user_email resolves the member - Email compares as new_user does, the SSO subject exactly - A value naming more than one account is refused with a 400 - That check covers an exact user id too, not just fallbacks - Removals resolve too, against the roster rather than the table ## User Flow Before: an admin who provisions people through OIDC SSO and pushes groups from the same identity provider cannot push a group at all 1. The person signs in through SSO, so their account exists on the gateway with their email and the provider's subject on it 2. The identity provider pushes the group with POST https://litellm-domain/scim/v2/Groups, listing that person by the id it uses for them, their email 3. The push comes back 500 with `Failed to create user 'them@example.com' while provisioning group membership.`, so the entire group fails, including every other member listed alongside them 4. The identity provider treats a 500 as transient and retries the same payload, which fails identically every time 5. GET https://litellm-domain/user/info?user_id=<the real account id> shows no teams, and the person opens the UI and sees none of the models the team grants 6. The same 500 comes back when the pushed id differs from the stored email only in capitalisation, which is what a directory sends when it reads a group back and re-pushes it 7. When the provider lists that person by the provider's subject instead, the push does return 201, but GET https://litellm-domain/user/info?user_id=<the real account id> still shows no teams: the team went to a second, empty account created for the raw subject 8. If two accounts on the gateway share the pushed email, the push comes back 500 as well, with nothing in the response saying which id was the problem or why 9. On a gateway where an earlier sync already created one of those empty accounts, every later push reports success and hands the team to that empty account again, so the same people keep going missing and nothing in the response says so After: the same pushes land the team on the person's real account, and any id that cannot be attributed to exactly one person is named in a 400 instead of being guessed at 1. The person signs in through SSO, so their account exists on the gateway with their email and the provider's subject on it 2. The identity provider pushes the group with POST https://litellm-domain/scim/v2/Groups, listing that person by the id it uses for them, their email 3. The push returns 201 with the member listed under the person's real account id 4. The identity provider has nothing to retry, and every other member listed alongside them lands too 5. GET https://litellm-domain/user/info?user_id=<the real account id> shows the new team, and the person opens the UI and sees the models the team grants 6. A pushed id that differs only in capitalisation lands on the same account, since the id is compared the way the gateway already compares an email before it accepts one 7. When the provider lists that person by the provider's subject instead, the push returns 201 under the same real account id, and GET https://litellm-domain/user/info?user_id=<the raw subject> returns 404, since no duplicate account was created 8. If two accounts share the pushed email, the push comes back 400 naming that id and saying it names more than one user, so the admin can fix the duplicate or send the user id instead. Neither account is handed access that was meant for the other 9. Later the provider takes that person out of the group with PATCH https://litellm-domain/scim/v2/Groups/<group id> and a `remove` naming the same email or subject it added them with, and GET https://litellm-domain/user/info?user_id=<the real account id> shows the team gone 10. On a gateway where an earlier sync already created one of those empty accounts, the push comes back 400 naming that id and saying an empty account provisioned under it should be deleted. The admin deletes it, re-runs the push, and the person lands on their real account. Until they do, that id keeps being refused rather than quietly handed to the empty account 11. When an id really does match nobody, the placeholder is still created as before, but a WARNING now names that id in the proxy logs so the admin can see it without turning on debug logging ## Relevant issues ## Linear ticket Resolves LIT-5383 ## Pre-Submission checklist - [x] I have added meaningful tests - [x] The handful of test files covering my change pass locally, e.g. `uv run pytest tests/test_litellm/<your_test_file>.py -v`. Leave the suites (`make test-unit-*`, `make test-unit`) to CI: it finishes in ~15 minutes where a laptop takes an hour or more - [x] My PR passes all required CI/CD checks (e.g., lint, schema.d.ts sync check, etc.) - [x] My PR's scope is as isolated as possible; it only solves 1 specific problem - [ ] I have received a Greptile **Confidence Score of at least 4/5** before requesting a maintainer review (Greptile reviews automatically once the PR is opened; only comment `@greptileai` to re-request a review after pushing changes) ## Screenshots / Proof of Fix Shared setup, identical for both legs. The only production file this PR touches is `litellm/proxy/management_endpoints/scim/scim_v2.py`, so each leg loads that file at the stated commit and prints its sha256 as provenance. Same proxy, same database, same script, same seven cases in the same order 1. `docker run -d --name lit5383-pg -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=litellm -e POSTGRES_USER=litellm -e POSTGRES_DB=litellm -p 5483:5432 postgres:16` 2. `DATABASE_URL="postgresql://litellm:litellm@localhost:5483/litellm" prisma db push --accept-data-loss --skip-generate` ```text Prisma schema loaded from schem